      "finalAnswer": "The core matching logic lives in **`Layer`** in `lib/router/layer.js`:\n\n## Regex compilation — `Layer` constructor\n[`lib/router/layer.js:45`](lib/router/layer.js) — the constructor compiles the path string into a regex at registration time (not per request), using the `path-to-regexp` package:\n\n```js\nthis.regexp = pathRegexp(path, this.keys = [], opts);\n```\n\nIt also pre-sets two fast-path flags on the compiled regex (lines 48–49): `fast_star` for `*` and `fast_slash` for `/` with `end === false`.\n\n## Match evaluation — `Layer.prototype.match(path)`\n[`lib/router/layer.js:110–156`](lib/router/layer.js) — **`Layer.prototype.match`** is the specific method that tests a layer path against an incoming request URL:\n\n1. Short-circuits on `fast_slash` (line 115) and `fast_star` (line 122)\n2. Executes the precompiled regex: `match = this.regexp.exec(path)` (line 129)\n3. On success, populates `this.params` (decoding captured groups via `decode_param`) and `this.path = match[0]`, then returns `true` (lines 138–155); returns `false` and clears state otherwise.\n\n## Call site in the routing loop\n`Router#handle` in [`lib/router/index.js:224–226`](lib/router/index.js) walks the router's layer stack per request:\n\n```js\nwhile (match !== true && idx < stack.length) {\n  layer = stack[idx++];\n  match = matchLayer(layer, path);   // → layer.match(path), wrapped in try/catch (line 583)\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nSo the full chain is: `Router#handle` → `matchLayer(layer, path)` (line 583) → **`Layer.prototype.match`** → `pathRegexp` (path-to-regexp) compiled once in the `Layer` constructor.",
